# What Happens When You Type https://www.google.com in Your Browser and Press Enter?

When a user types **https://www.google.com** into a web browser and presses Enter, several components work together to deliver the requested web page.

## 1. DNS Resolution

The browser first needs to find Google's IP address. It sends a DNS query to a DNS server, which translates the domain name (**[www.google.com](http://www.google.com)**) into an IP address.

## 2. Establishing a Secure Connection

Using the returned IP address, the browser initiates an HTTPS connection on **port 443**. HTTPS uses **TLS/SSL encryption** to secure the communication between the browser and Google's servers.

## 3. Firewall Inspection

The request passes through a firewall, which filters malicious or unauthorized traffic before allowing the request to continue.

## 4. Load Balancing

The request reaches a load balancer. The load balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple servers to improve performance, reliability, and availability.

## 5. Web Server

The selected web server receives the request and determines how it should be handled.

## 6. Application Server

The web server forwards the request to an application server. The application server contains the business logic required to process the request.

## 7. Database Query

If data is needed, the application server sends a query to a database. The database retrieves the requested information and returns it to the application server.

## 8. Generating the Response

The application server generates the web page and sends it back to the web server.

## 9. Returning the Web Page

The web server sends the encrypted response back to the browser, which renders the page for the user.

